iRiel System must communicate relevant system state to the human.
A human should not be required to infer whether the system is processing, waiting, unavailable, or completed when that distinction materially affects the interaction.
System feedback should be accessible through the appropriate modality.
For example:
Visual users may receive visual state changes.
Voice-first users may receive audio feedback.
Users requiring haptic feedback may receive appropriate tactile confirmation.
Text-based users may receive explicit textual state.
The same system state should be expressible through multiple modalities where appropriate.
Accessibility is therefore part of the system architecture.
